1)Deadlock Debugging:
   Condition Causing Deadlock
  Two processes (P1 and P2) share two resources (R1 and R2).
   P1 locks R1 first and then tries to lock R2.
   P2 locks R2 first and then tries to lock R1.
   After waiting for 100 ms, both processes request the second resource.
   P1 cannot acquire R2 because it is already locked by P2.
   P2 cannot acquire R1 because it is already locked by P1.
 
    lock(R1)
    lock(R2)
    Critical Section
    unlock(R2)
    unlock(R1)
    Process P2:
    lock(R1)
    lock(R2)
    Critical Section
     unlock(R2)
     unlock(R1)
     Optimization to Eliminate Deadlock :
      Follow a fixed resource ordering.
    Both processes should lock resources in the same order (R1 → R2).
    This removes the circular wait condition.
    Deadlock is avoided without changing the process logic.
Cause: Circular wait due to different lock order.
Solution: Use the same resource locking order for all processes.

2)Cause of Low CPU Utilization
CPU utilization is only 30% even though processes are ready.
The scheduler log shows repeated transitions:
Ready → Waiting → Ready → Waiting → Ready
This indicates that processes are I/O-bound.
Processes execute for a short time and then wait for I/O operations.
While waiting for I/O, the CPU remains idle.
Frequent state changes increase scheduling overhead.
System-Level Optimization
Increase the degree of multiprogramming so another process can use the CPU while one is waiting.
Use asynchronous or buffered I/O to reduce waiting time.
Improve CPU scheduling by selecting CPU-bound processes when I/O-bound processes are waiting.
Reduce unnecessary context switching.
Optimize I/O devices to decrease waiting time.
Result
CPU remains busy most of the time.CPU utilization increases.Throughput improves.Overall system performance becomes better.

