4)Root Cause of Starvation
The scheduler uses a Multilevel Queue Scheduling algorithm.
There are three queues:
Q1: Interactive (Highest Priority)
Q2: System
Q3: Batch (FCFS)
Process P5 is in Q3 (Batch Queue).
The CPU always gives priority to Q1 and Q2 processes.
Whenever a process arrives in Q1 or Q2, P5 is postponed.
As a result, P5 waits for a very long time without getting CPU time.
This condition is called Starvation (Indefinite Blocking).
The process remains ready but is never executed.
Optimization to Ensure Fairness
Use the Aging Technique.
Gradually increase the priority of processes waiting for a long time.
Move P5 from Q3 to a higher-priority queue after a certain waiting time.
This guarantees that every process eventually gets CPU time.
Aging removes starvation and improves fairness.
Result
Starvation is eliminated.All processes receive CPU time.System fairness and performance improve.

5). Context-Switch Overhead Debugging 
Cause of High Context Switching
The server performs 20,000 context switches per second.
The average CPU burst time is 2 ms.
The scheduler time quantum is only 1 ms.
Processes are preempted before completing their CPU burst.
Very frequent I/O operations cause processes to enter the waiting state repeatedly.
Each preemption and I/O completion results in another context switch.
Excessive context switching wastes CPU time and increases overhead.
This reduces system performance and throughput.
Use efficient thread scheduling to minimize switching overhead.

Result
Number of context switches decreases.CPU overhead is reduced.CPU utilization increases.System throughput and overall performance improve.
